//     P9, gut-only rows: the Cmax ratio is NOT close to the AUC ratio (predicted): the gut tissue releases the spared drug
//       slowly, so AUC rises more than Cmax: diltiazem 1.35 (predicted 1.55) PASS; ciclosporin 1.96 against 2.16 PASS
//       (predicted 3.1, FAIL: wrong).
//     P9 ceilings: the gut wall also clears ARTERIAL drug (Q_gut Eg = 33 % of the blood clearance), which the hand
//       estimate ignored: gut fully inhibited gives AUC x 10.4 (predicted 6.9), the liver alone at most x 3.1. Ketoconazole
//       (x 10.9) is 5 % above the gut ceiling; with the hepatic factor 0.95 added: Cmax x 2.94 against 4.3 (ratio 0.68, FAIL;
//       predicted 6.5, the wrong side) and CL/CL' 1.55 against an unchanged half-life (FAIL as predicted).
//     P9 rifampicin: same factor on gut and liver gives Cmax x 0.42 against 0.29 (FAIL, ratio 1.46; predicted 0.20, the
//       wrong side); gut-only induction (x 6.0) gives 0.24 (PASS, no prediction had been written); liver only 0.73 FAIL.
//     P9 verapamil and erythromycin: the header compared SINGLE-DOSE Cmax ratios with studies made at STEADY STATE (my
//       error, found after the first run: single-dose ratios 1.61 and 2.17). Rows replaced by steady-state runs, disclosed:
//       verapamil Cmax,ss x 1.85 against 2.3 PASS (ratio 0.80); erythromycin x 2.95 against 4.4 FAIL (ratio 0.67).
//   WHICH NEED THE GUT PATHWAY: all of them. Liver-only factors leave Cmax nearly unchanged (diltiazem 1.03, rifampicin
//     0.73) and cannot reach AUC x 3.3, x 4.2 or x 10.9 at all.
//   BUGS OF MINE found and fixed before the bands were set (no tuning): the standard grid had no sample at 24 h (C24 and
//     AUC(0-24) printed 0); a factor 0.001 too many in two fit-check rows; two fit rounds left R24 3 % off (three rounds: 0.5 %).
//   RUNTIME: compiled 3.8 s (fit 3 rounds on coarse grids, 4 h runs for interaction Cmax, 16-dose steady states on a 17
